WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.673
Apollo Q Labs. Level three, module one: delta and gamma.

2
00:00:05.973 --> 00:00:11.180
Delta measures direction: the change in option price for a one dollar move in the stock.

3
00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:21.805
Calls, the right to buy 100 shares at the strike, range from zero to plus one, and puts, the right to sell 100 shares at the strike, from zero to minus one.

4
00:00:21.805 --> 00:00:38.447
At the money options, with a strike, the price the option lets you buy or sell at, near the stock price, sit near plus or minus point five. Deep in the money options, worth a lot if used now, sit near one. Far out of the money options, worth nothing if used now, sit near zero.

5
00:00:38.447 --> 00:00:44.720
Selling flips the sign: short calls, calls you sold, have negative delta, and short puts, positive.

6
00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:59.635
Many traders also read delta as a rough chance of finishing in the money. A point three delta call is roughly a thirty percent shot, a rough rule of thumb. The dashboard’s probability of profit is a different calculation.

7
00:00:59.635 --> 00:01:02.751
But in the money is not the same as profitable.

8
00:01:03.051 --> 00:01:09.836
Example: a twenty dollar call, meaning its strike is twenty dollars, priced at two dollars, with delta point five.

9
00:01:09.836 --> 00:01:17.624
The stock rises one dollar, so the call is about two fifty. On one contract, one hundred shares, that is plus fifty dollars.

10
00:01:17.924 --> 00:01:24.155
Gamma is how fast delta changes: the change in delta for a one dollar move in the stock.

11
00:01:24.155 --> 00:01:36.679
Gamma is largest for at the money options close to expiration, the option’s last day. That is why short dated trades, with little time left, can swing from winning to losing quickly.

12
00:01:36.679 --> 00:01:43.635
Buyers have positive gamma, so moves help them. Sellers have negative gamma, so big moves hurt them.

13
00:01:43.935 --> 00:01:51.787
Example: delta point five, gamma point oh five. The stock rises one dollar, so delta becomes about point five five.

14
00:01:52.087 --> 00:02:04.952
Apollo Q Labs shows Greeks, these measures of how an option reacts, for your whole position: the per share Greek, times one hundred, times the number of contracts, with sells counted negative.

15
00:02:04.952 --> 00:02:12.185
Add them across legs, the separate options in your trade, to see the net exposure, your overall sensitivity.

16
00:02:12.185 --> 00:02:17.584
Greeks change constantly as price and time move, so treat them as a snapshot.

17
00:02:17.884 --> 00:02:27.741
Recap: delta is how much the option price moves for a one dollar stock move, gamma is how fast delta changes, and position Greeks add up your whole trade.

18
00:02:27.741 --> 00:02:31.348
Next, read the lessons, then take the six question quiz.

